Tremendous public service from Binance to publish this. 

Take your time to read it. Make all your engineers read it. In fact, anyone you know who works in tech, have them read it.

This was the largest single heist in human history, $6B+, and it happened through mundane channels.

The Internet is now going to be full of AI-accelerated social engineering toxic sludge, and it's your job to be hypervigilant. Build your infrastructure in a way that assumes it will get compromised. Make sure you have AI-powered monitoring 24/7 and a "shut it all down" killswitch in case you ever need to go nuclear. The stakes are too high to be scrambling in the moment the way it seems Binance was during this attack.

@0xAbhiP The attack was spread across an hour

@CryptoHulkemist Exactly. They did not have a kill switch that encompassed all wallets.

They also didn't mention the auto-refilling from cold wallets, which continued for a while into the attack.

@tudoratu A killswitch for wallet infrastructure is probably not "kill the authorization service," it's transferring all assets to a cold safe address.

First two are indeed stupid, but as an employer you have to model for this happening.

"AI agent having a crack at the assignment," I don't think that's what happened. It sounds more like their own internal AI background service had some kind of pub-sub service across machines that they were able to move laterally through? But unclear.

@EvgenyGaevoy @eli0xeli Yes, but better endpoint detection should have stopped this. The fact that this script was writing to pyenv and wasn't caught by EDR is crazy, and probably means they need more EDR coverage (or just better EDR)

@LorenzoARK Disagree. There is nothing the blockchain layer would be able to identify this is as a hack. That is only possible knowing the semantics of Binance's infrastructure, which means it's on Binance to specify that in their own infra, not the blockchain itself.

EDR is basically just anomaly detection, and I don't think that's true at all. At the end of the day, having active monitoring on your own computer to see "is there weird thing happening in sensitive directories that shouldn't be happening" is a solved problem.

In this case for DevOps engineers, I guarantee you that going forward, they will not be able to run ANY binaries that are not approved by IT. In which case this category of attack will no longer be possible. And any machine that writes to pyenv or hooks into random .dlls will suddenly get taken offline.

A lot of this stuff gets calibrated based on employees complaining "this is annoying, it's too sensitive, it's too trigger-happy, I can't do my job." After you get hacked for billions, you stop caring about that and default to extremely aggressive settings for your org.

Security everywhere is like this, and I bet that's a lot of the takeaway for Binance internally.

This case is a perfect example of that; this was not a 0-day. There was no fancy compromise. It's all very vanilla stuff that aggressively-configured EDR should've caught.

There is no software that can comprehensively defend against 0-days, but these were more or less known malware routes. 99% of attacks are like this.

